"Population & Migration | Economic Research Service (https://www.ers.usda.gov/topics/rural-economy-population/population-migration)\nciteturn19view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ers.usda.gov/topics/rural-economy-population/population-migration\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 166\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL3: \nL4: 【1†Economic Research Service U.S. DEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE】\nL5: \nL6: [Button: Menu]\nL7: \nL8: 【2†Image】\nL9: \nL10:   * 【3†Topics】\nL11:   * Data\nL12: \nL13: Access the Data\nL14: \nL15: Access and analyze data sets, maps, charts and graphs.\nL16: \nL17: 【4†Browse All Data】\nL18: \nL19:     * Data by Type\nL20: \nL21: Access the Data\nL22: \nL23:     * 【5†Charts】\nL24:     * 【6†Downloadable Data Files】\nL25:     * 【7†Queryable Data Applications】\nL26:     * 【8†Maps】\nL27: \nL28:     * Data by Topic\nL29: \nL30: Explore the Data Repository\nL31:     * 【9†Animal Products】\nL32:     * 【10†Crops】\nL33:     * 【11†Farm Bill】\nL34:     * 【12†Farm Economy】\nL35:     * 【13†Farm Practices & Management】\nL36:     * 【14†Food & Nutrition Assistance】\nL37:     * 【15†Food Choices & Health】\nL38:     * 【16†Food Markets & Prices】\nL39:     * 【17†Food Safety】\nL40:     * 【18†International Markets & U.S. Trade】\nL41:     * 【19†Natural Resources & Environment】\nL42:     * 【20†Rural Economy & Population】\nL43: \nL44:   * Analysis\nL45: \nL46: Understand the Research\nL47: \nL48: Get insights and objective analysis from experts in the field.\nL49: \nL50: 【21†Browse All Analysis】\nL51:     * 【22†Research Reports Published Papers from ERS】\nL52:     * 【23†Outlook Reports Agricultural Outlooks and Projections】\nL53: \nL54:     * 【24†Amber Waves The Economics of Food, Farming, Natural Resources, and Rural Amer…】\nL55:     * 【25†Charts of Note Fast facts on U.S. agriculture】\nL56: \nL57:   * Newsroom\nL58: \nL59: Newsroom\nL60: \nL61: See what's new and what's trending from ERS.\nL62: \nL63: 【26†Visit the Newsroom】\nL64: \nL65:     * 【27†Events】\nL66:     * 【28†Trending Topics】\nL67:     * 【29†Contact ERS Press Office】\nL68: \nL69:     * 【30†Subject Specialists】\nL70: \nL71:   * 【24†Amber Waves】\nL72:   * 【31†Calendar】\nL73:   * 【32†About】\nL74:   * 【33†Careers】\nL75:   * 【34†Contact Us】\nL76: \nL77: [Input] Search [Input] 【35†Image: Search】\nL78: # Population & Migration\nL79: \nL80:   * Updated: 2/13/2026\nL81:   * Contact: 【36†Justin B. Winikoff】\nL82: \nL83: [Button: In this section]\nL84: \nL85:   * 【37†Population & Migration】\nL86: After a decade of population loss in the 2010s, the population in nonmetropolitan (nonmetro) areas has increased each year since 2020. The growth is from migration, which has offset a population loss in nonmetro areas caused by more deaths than births. Between 2020 and 2021, 92 percent of net migration to nonmetro counties was domestic (within the United States). However, between 2021 and 2024, domestic net migration decreased while international migration increased in nonmetro counties.\nL87: About half (48 percent) of net migration gains in nonmetro counties between 2023 and 2024 were because of international migration. ERS tracks demographic changes in nonmetro areas and conducts research to help explain the relationship between population changes and the well-being of rural and small-town residents. This page summarizes key trends in the nonmetro population change:\nL88:   * 【38†Population growth and decline】\nL89:   * 【39†Nonmetro counties see more deaths than births】\nL90:   * 【40†Migration】\nL91:   * 【41†Age structure】\nL92: #### Population Growth and Decline\nL93: The total U.S. population in nonmetro counties stood at 46.2 million in July 2024. It constitutes 14 percent of U.S. residents spread across 72 percent of the nation's land area. The number of people living in nonmetro counties increased by 134,540 (0.29 percent) between 2023 and 2024. Still, the population in metropolitan (metro) areas continues to grow faster (1.1 percent).\nL94: The metro and nonmetro growth rates were similar in 2020–21 during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic but the difference in growth rates has widened again.\nL95: 【42†Image: Line chart showing that the population in metropolitan areas continues to grow faster than in nonmetropolitan areas.】\nL96: \nL97: 【43†Download chart image】 | 【44†Chart data】\nL98: Population change varies widely across rural and small-town America. Recent growth (2020–24) has been concentrated in counties adjacent to metro areas and counties with recreation economies, with most other nonmetro counties seeing decline. Despite an overall nonmetro population increase, 51 percent of nonmetro counties saw population declines between July 2020 and June 2024.\nL99: A nonmetro population decline was common across much of West Virginia, Pennsylvania, the Dakotas, Nebraska, Kansas, Illinois, Iowa, and California. Decline was also common in western Oklahoma and western Texas, the Mississippi Delta region of Mississippi, Arkansas and Louisiana; southern Alabama, and Appalachian Kentucky.\nL100: Population growth was common in nonmetro counties across the Rocky Mountain region (especially in Idaho, Utah, Washington, Montana, and Wyoming); the northern parts of Minnesota, Wisconsin, and Michigan; the Texas hill country; parts of Maine, Vermont, and New Hampshire; the Missouri Ozarks; and across Tennessee, Florida, and North Carolina.\nL101: 【45†Image: Map of U.S. counties showing that population change varies widely across rural and small-town America with growth concentrated in counties adjacent to metropolitan areas and counties with recreation economies】\nL102: \nL103: 【46†Download chart image】\nL104: #### Nonmetropolitan Counties See More Deaths Than Births\nL105: Population change occurs from a combination of natural change (births minus deaths) and net migration (in-migration minus out-migration). Falling birth rates and an aging population have steadily reduced the U.S. population growth from natural change in nonmetro counties over time. Each year since 2017, nonmetro areas have recorded more deaths than births (natural decrease). Between 2020 and 2024, a natural decrease reduced the nonmetro population by 563,550 people.\nL106: Natural decrease is now widespread across nonmetro counties, with 1,492 nonmetro counties (76 percent) seeing a natural decrease between July 2023 and June 2024. Natural decrease should be expected to continue because of population aging.\nL107: \nL108: 【47†Image: Line graph showing nonmetropolitan natural increases (births minus deaths) and net migration (in migration minus out migration). Natural decrease is now widespread across nonmetropolitan counties. Recent growth has been due to migration.】\nL109: 【48†Download chart image】 | 【49†Chart data】\nL110: #### Migration\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPoverty rates highest for rural children under 5 years old in 2023 | Economic Research Service (https://www.ers.usda.gov/data-products/charts-of-note/110356)\nciteturn19view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ers.usda.gov/data-products/charts-of-note/110356\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 135\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL3: \nL4: 【1†Economic Research Service U.S. DEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE】\nL5: \nL6: [Button: Menu]\nL7: \nL8: 【2†Image】\nL9: \nL10:   * 【3†Topics】\nL11:   * Data\nL12: \nL13: Access the Data\nL14: \nL15: Access and analyze data sets, maps, charts and graphs.\nL16: \nL17: 【4†Browse All Data】\nL18: \nL19:     * Data by Type\nL20: \nL21: Access the Data\nL22: \nL23:     * 【5†Charts】\nL24:     * 【6†Downloadable Data Files】\nL25:     * 【7†Queryable Data Applications】\nL26:     * 【8†Maps】\nL27: \nL28:     * Data by Topic\nL29: \nL30: Explore the Data Repository\nL31:     * 【9†Animal Products】\nL32:     * 【10†Crops】\nL33:     * 【11†Farm Bill】\nL34:     * 【12†Farm Economy】\nL35:     * 【13†Farm Practices & Management】\nL36:     * 【14†Food & Nutrition Assistance】\nL37:     * 【15†Food Choices & Health】\nL38:     * 【16†Food Markets & Prices】\nL39:     * 【17†Food Safety】\nL40:     * 【18†International Markets & U.S. Trade】\nL41:     * 【19†Natural Resources & Environment】\nL42:     * 【20†Rural Economy & Population】\nL43: \nL44:   * Analysis\nL45: \nL46: Understand the Research\nL47: \nL48: Get insights and objective analysis from experts in the field.\nL49: \nL50: 【21†Browse All Analysis】\nL51:     * 【22†Research Reports Published Papers from ERS】\nL52:     * 【23†Outlook Reports Agricultural Outlooks and Projections】\nL53: \nL54:     * 【24†Amber Waves The Economics of Food, Farming, Natural Resources, and Rural Amer…】\nL55:     * 【25†Charts of Note Fast facts on U.S. agriculture】\nL56: \nL57:   * Newsroom\nL58: \nL59: Newsroom\nL60: \nL61: See what's new and what's trending from ERS.\nL62: \nL63: 【26†Visit the Newsroom】\nL64: \nL65:     * 【27†Events】\nL66:     * 【28†Trending Topics】\nL67:     * 【29†Contact ERS Press Office】\nL68: \nL69:     * 【30†Subject Specialists】\nL70: \nL71:   * 【24†Amber Waves】\nL72:   * 【31†Calendar】\nL73:   * 【32†About】\nL74:   * 【33†Careers】\nL75:   * 【34†Contact Us】\nL76: \nL77: [Input] Search [Input] 【35†Image: Search】\nL78: # Poverty rates highest for rural children under 5 years old in 2023\nL79: \nL80:   * by 【36†Tracey Farrigan】\nL81:   * 11/13/2024\nL82: \nL83: 【37†Image: Vertical bar chart showing poverty rate by age group and nonmetro/metro residence in 2023.】\nL84: \nL85: 【38†Download chart image】\nL86: The poverty rate for rural children under 5 years old was the highest of any age group in both rural and urban areas, standing at 20.9 percent. The poverty status of children is based on their family’s total income, so it is typically correlated with labor force characteristics of the parent(s).\nL87: Young adults often have low levels of education, may be enrolled in school, and may be early in their career or have limited time in the workforce, which may restrict their ability as parents to earn enough to be above the poverty level. In addition, workers in many rural areas face limited job opportunities and lower wages relative to urban areas. Single parenthood (only one potential earner) and lack of childcare can also inhibit parental engagement in the labor force.\nL88: The poverty rate—the portion of the population living below the official poverty level—for the rural population in general was 13.6 percent (about 6 million people) in 2023 compared with 10.7 percent for the urban population. About 46 million people lived in rural counties in 2023, roughly 14 percent of the U.S. population. This figure appears in 【39†Rural America at a Glance: 2024 Edition】, published in November 2024.\nL89: ## Related Content\nL90: \nL91: ### [Button: Topics]\nL92: \nL93:   * 【40†Rural Economy & Population】\nL94:   * 【41†Rural Poverty & Well-Being】\nL95: \nL96: ### [Button: Data]\nL97: \nL98:   * 【42†Poverty Area Measures】\nL99:   * 【43†County Typology Codes】\nL100: \nL101: Get Charts of Note delivered!\nL102: \nL103: 【44†Subscribe†visitor.r20.constantcontact.com】\nL104: \nL105: See our 【45†Privacy Policy】.\nL106: \nL107: 【46†Return to top】\nL108: \nL109: #### ERS Links\nL110: \nL111:   * 【47†Accessibility】\nL112:   * 【33†Careers】\nL113:   * 【34†Contact Us】\nL114:   * 【48†Help】\nL115:   * 【49†Information Quality】\nL116:   * 【45†Privacy Policy】\nL117:   * 【50†Site Map】\nL118: #### USDA Links\nL119: \nL120:   * 【51†FOIA】\nL121:   * 【52†Non-Discrimination Statement†www.usda.gov】\nL122:   * 【53†Plain Writing†www.usda.gov】\nL123:   * 【54†Report Fraud†usdaoig.oversight.gov】\nL124:   * 【55†USDA.gov†www.usda.gov】\nL125:   * 【56†USA.gov†www.usa.gov】\nL126:   * 【57†White House.gov†www.whitehouse.gov】\nL127: \nL128: ### ERS E-newsletters\nL129: \nL130: 【44†Subscribe†visitor.r20.constantcontact.com】\nL131: \nL132: 【58†Image: U.S. Department